                        "entry_id": "a12ed807-a83e-3ee5-885d-98eb7f7738be",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Originally, a three-legged decorative stand or table; now, especially, one with recesses for holding tea caddies and/or a tea service."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "c67c199a-c8a9-48a3-a55d-f73918e8d156",
                                "sense_id": "0d3f52b2-ecbf-3e5c-b115-6760d28717ba",
                                "text": "“They're like the teapoys in the Lakka Bazar — good material but not polished.”"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "7d43a14a-406b-4e38-8a3f-46307a0c2830",
                                "sense_id": "0d3f52b2-ecbf-3e5c-b115-6760d28717ba",
                                "text": "The teapoy, derived from the Hindi/Persian phrase denoting a three-footed table, supported a tea set or tea-related objects. Not all teapoys were tripod-like tables or sold singly. The Carneses imported lacquer teapoys in sets. These sets could be easily stacked in a corner of the drawing room and brought out at teatime to hold a teacup, a set, or a caddy. The Carneses purchased lacquered teapoys sets for four dollars in China and probably sold them for twice that amount in America."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
